<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Chu</h1><p class="st-meta">206 BCE – 202 BCE · capital Pengcheng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Xiang Yu, Hegemon-King of Western Chu, who destroyed the Qin armies and redrew China's map, only to lose it all to Liu Bang at Gaixia in 202 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>, grandson of the Chu general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yan">Xiang Yan</a>, rose with his uncle Xiang <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-wei">Liang</a> in the rebellion against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>. After his victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-julu">Julu</a> in 207 BCE he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>, killed the surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziying">Ziying</a> and burned the palaces. In 206 BCE he divided the empire into eighteen kingdoms and took for himself the title of Hegemon-King of Western Chu, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Pengcheng</a> (Xuzhou).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Western Chu held nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> of the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-wei">State of Wei</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> dominated the other kings by military force, but he alienated allies, had his nominal sovereign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yi-of-chu">Emperor Yi of Chu</a> killed in 205 BCE and treated defeated enemies brutally. His crushing victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Pengcheng</a> was never turned into lasting advantage.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Over four years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-han-contention">Chu-Han war</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang's</a> alliances wore Western Chu down. Surrounded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaixia">Gaixia</a> in 202 BCE and hearing Chu songs from the enemy camp, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> broke out and killed himself by the Wu River. Liu Bang then founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/western-chu.jpg" alt="Western Chu" width="320" height="341"><figcaption>Image: Jiu Qiaozai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%88%98%E5%9B%BD%E5%BD%A2%E5%8A%BF%E5%9B%BE%EF%BC%88%E5%89%8D350%E5%B9%B4%EF%BC%89%EF%BC%88%E7%AE%80%EF%BC%89.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>206 BCE – 202 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pengcheng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Xiang Yu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Western Chu (Xiang Yu)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-204&amp;focus=western-chu" aria-label="Western Chu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7589 1068 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Western Chu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7909 1324L7768 1187M7909 1324L7769 1191M7909 1324L7739 1235"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1187h0M7769 1191h0M7739 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7909 1324h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7909 1324h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-204&amp;focus=western-chu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
